n f(t) return walk(t) end\\n'\n    local _, sw = MX.mix(assert(R.read(W, 'lua')), 'f', { 'D' }, {})\n    ok((kinds(sw).generalize or 0) >= 1, vim.inspect(kinds(sw)))\n    local okw = pcall(MX.mix, assert(R.read(W, 'lua')), 'f', { 'D' }, {}, { decide = function (kind) if kind == 'generalize' then return false end end })\n    eq(false, okw, 'vetoed: refused')","file":"tests/mix_spec.lua"},"verb":"edit","touched":["tests/mix_spec.lua"]},"id":"1791247107.131528-edit"},{"bytes":1458,"decided_by":"tactic","decisions":[],"verb":"edit","invocation":{"args":{"before":"| Can a derivation be specialized THROUGH its basis — `B.join` followed into the algebra's own code, so mix specializes it on a static template instead of calling it opaque? | `MA.program(key, nil, { snapshot = true, through = true [, opaque = { ['M.x'] = true }] })` (CART-1500): a captured table's FIELD (`B.join`) or a captured ALIAS (`local label = M.node_label`) whose value IS a definition of the program's files is followed into the closure and rewritten to its mangled name; the algebra's DATA fields the followed code reads (`M.OBSERVED`) become knowns; `opaque` keeps named definitions as primitives (a NARROWED domain). D.match through the basis: 84 definitions (64 from the algebra), every basis call followed, lowers with nothing refused. Mix gaps it surfaced, fixed: a callee's closure-stored parameter now forces the CALLER's variable dynamic (a `local ids = {}` handed to `content_id` — never a shared constant); a closure that keeps growing is retried DYNAMIC on a depth refusal too (a growing continuation `g(x-1, function (y) return k(y) + 1 end)` now specializes, equal to the original). Then (2026-10-06): a SINGLE-VALUED call as an expanding last argument — a named program function or a statically known closure that returns one value on every path — fills one parameter and leaves the rest nil (rung 3 for that case: `M.rep(M.join_domain(…))`); a static NIL embeds every value in the whistle (`put(t, path, sub