value in the whistle (`put(t, path, sub)` then `i = i or 1`: the index generalizes instead of 120 program points). RESULT, D.match through the basis with `admits`/`admits_slice`/`match`/`entails` OPAQUE (narrowed): it specializes (4,801 residual lines), equal to the derivation on every sample; fair timing (fresh process each, interleaved, median of 5) us/call: derived 50.2, compiled with the basis opaque 65.3, THROUGH the basis 38.7, hand 15.4 — 1.3x faster than the derivation, 2.5x from hand-written. OPEN: through `admits` (not opaque) the unfold explodes the budget. `tests/mixalg_spec.lua`, `tests/mix_spec.lua`. CART-1500 |\n| What did the SPECIALIZER decide, and what if one decision went the other way? (interaction points in an autonomous compiler) | The DECISION HOOK (CART-1501): `MX.specialize(prog, f, division, statics, { decide = function (kind, ctx, default) … end })` — answering nil keeps the default (today's rule; a run without the hook is unchanged), any other answer replaces it; every decision is LOGGED in `res.decisions` (`MX.mix`: `stats.decisions`) as `{ kind, ctx, default, choice }`. Kinds: `fold` (a pure primitive computed on static arguments; ctx.prim), `unfold` (a one-return callee substituted, or kept a residual function), `generalize` (the whistle's answer — `{ [param] = true }` to make dynamic, false = refuse; ctx.refusal, ctx.ancestor), `grow` (a growing continuation retried with its closures dynamic; ctx.closures). ctx: `fn`, `division`, `at` (source position through the line map), `chain` (active calls, innermost first). mixalg through mode adds `follow` (`MA.program(…, { through = true, decide = … })`, `report.decisions`; ctx.from, ctx.name, ctx.target), asked once per site; a run with a hook bypasses the program cache. NOT YET: policies over the hook — a recorded plan replayed, an interactive driver, an oracle trying both; today's only alternative policy is `opts.reuse = 'eager'`. Overhead: none measured (33 compiled matchers, 4.84 s vs 4.